"""Formal company and product narratives for the researched second edition.

Claims describe the inspected application. No customer, certification, scale or
deployment claims are inferred from demonstration data.
"""

def article(title, lead, blocks, label='Company and product'):
    return dict(kind='article', title=title, lead=lead, blocks=blocks, label=label)

COMPANY = [
article('Company overview',
    'Weirwood develops Mosaic, an investigation and intelligence workspace that brings case records, analytical tools and accountable decisions into a common operating context.', [
    ('Product focus', 'Mosaic addresses the work required to understand records distributed across sources. An investigation may involve people, organisations, financial accounts, communications, vehicles, travel and places. Each source describes only part of that activity. The product connects those records so an authorised officer can examine a relationship, inspect its basis and carry the relevant material into a case workflow.'),
    ('Institutional purpose', 'The intended environment is a public institution in which access, responsibility and the handling of records must remain explicit. Mosaic models agency, role, case and purpose alongside the investigative functions. The company’s product approach therefore combines analysis with the controls required to review an action, preserve its rationale and inspect the resulting record of activity.'),
    ('Scope of the offering', 'The workspace covers data preparation, entity resolution, search, dossiers, network analysis, financial tracing, geographic examination, leads, evidence and reporting. Supervisory review, audit inspection and administration operate within the same product model. A separate disaster-response view organises ingested alerts, affected districts and recorded resource allocations for control-room work.'),
    ('Product responsibility', 'The application supports an officer’s examination of evidence; it does not confer investigative authority or establish a finding by itself. Source owners remain responsible for their records, authorised personnel make decisions and the institution defines its operational arrangements. This document presents the implemented product and its synthetic demonstration. It does not assert a customer base, institutional endorsement, production deployment or measured operational outcome.')]),
article('The institutional problem',
    'The principal challenge is continuity: maintaining the connection between a source record, its interpretation, the decision it informs and the material eventually presented for review.', [
    ('Fragmented information', 'A bank extract, a call record, an incident report and a vehicle sighting can refer to the same person or event without sharing a common format. Officers must reconcile identifiers, dates, spelling variations and source conventions before they can assess the relationship. Repeating this preparation in disconnected files makes it harder to retain the provenance of each conclusion.'),
    ('Identity and interpretation', 'Names alone are often insufficient to establish that two records describe the same individual. Original scripts, transliterations, initials, family information and incomplete identifiers require contextual comparison. A proposed match must distinguish the evidence that agrees from the evidence that conflicts. Similarly, a financial or movement pattern requires examination of the underlying records and plausible alternative explanations.'),
    ('Review and handover', 'Investigations depend on coordinated work between officers and supervisors. A request needs its purpose, relevant records and decision history. An exhibit needs its source and custody events. A report needs a clear separation between recorded facts and the officer’s interpretation. When these elements are separated, the reviewer has to reconstruct the reasoning before assessing the proposed action.'),
    ('Mosaic’s response', 'Mosaic places these activities within a case context. It preserves links from analytical views to records, provides structured review steps and records the actor and reason for sensitive actions. The intended benefit is a more coherent working process: officers can move between preparation, examination, review and reporting while retaining the references needed to assess the work.')]),
article('Product architecture and scope',
    'Mosaic is organised around a connected record model. The same authorised objects can be examined as a dossier, a network, a financial path, a timeline or a geographic view.', [
    ('Objects and relationships', 'The model represents people, companies, accounts, phones, vehicles, locations, documents and events. Relationships express connections supported by the available material, such as ownership, a transfer, a call or a recorded observation. The usefulness of a connection depends on its source and evidence status. A machine suggestion, a single-source assertion and corroborating records must be interpreted in their respective contexts.'),
    ('Investigation workspace', 'Search and Explorer locate records. Dossiers consolidate relevant information about a selected object. Network, Connections, Money trail and Map answer different questions about the relationships around it. Leads, alerts and watches identify material requiring attention. The active case supplies the common context as the officer moves between these tools.'),
    ('Case and record operations', 'Case management records the purpose, legal basis, team, leads and outcomes of an investigation. Data studio prepares incoming material and submits proposed changes for separate approval. Evidence handling connects files to fingerprints and custody events. The report editor combines structured case facts with authored analysis, then produces a fixed export for a named release context.'),
    ('Governance and administration', 'Role and case restrictions, protected fields, review requirements and audit events are integral to the operating model. Oversight can inspect activity without receiving the investigative workspace. Administrators can examine documented detectors and source-quality issues. These functions support institutional operation; deployment configuration, source agreements and organisational procedures remain part of implementation.')]),
article('The operating workflow',
    'The product supports a sequence from receipt of information to reviewed action. Each stage produces a record that the next stage can examine, rather than requiring the team to recreate its context.', [
    ('01  Establish and prepare', 'An officer selects or opens a case with a purpose and recorded basis. Incoming extracts are profiled, cleaned and mapped into the record model. Data studio displays matching evidence, conflicts and the operations proposed for publication. Another eligible supervisor reviews the frozen proposal, the associated order and the proposer’s note before approving or rejecting it.'),
    ('02  Examine and develop', 'The officer locates a record, reviews its dossier and follows relevant relationships. Financial, communication, geographic and network views expose different aspects of the available material. A detector lead or alert provides a starting point for examination. The officer checks source records, time boundaries and alternative explanations before developing a line of enquiry.'),
    ('03  Coordinate and decide', 'Case leads progress through explicit states, including a supervisory authorisation stage. Watches and saved searches return newly available material to the work queue. Proposed record matches and face-search candidates receive human review. Each workflow retains the distinction between a suggestion, an officer’s decision and the status of subsequent work.'),
    ('04  Preserve and account', 'Files are sealed as exhibits with fingerprints and custody history. Reports combine selected facts, references and officer commentary, while release actions identify the recipient and clearance. Closure records the case outcome; retention and legal holds govern subsequent handling. Oversight reviews recorded access and decisions across this process, including refused requests.')]),
article('Roles and responsibilities',
    'Mosaic separates the responsibilities of preparing information, investigating it, authorising actions and inspecting system use. Available screens and operations follow the acting identity and the case context.', [
    ('Investigating officers and analysts', 'Officers use the workspace to examine records, develop leads, preserve exhibits and prepare case reports. Analysts can compare relationships, paths and proposed identity matches within their permitted context. Their work records what was examined and why it was relevant. Analytical priority or similarity remains a basis for review, rather than an automatic determination about a person.'),
    ('Supervisors', 'Supervisors review eligible case requests, proposed intakes and authorisation steps. The applicable workflow checks role, agency, case membership and separation from the proposer. A recorded approval or rejection communicates the decision to the requesting officer and preserves its reason. Supervisory access is therefore exercised through specific decisions, rather than treated as an unrestricted bypass of the operating model.'),
    ('Oversight and administration', 'Independent oversight examines activity, purpose, outcomes and audit integrity. Its role does not include the ordinary investigative record workspace. Administrators inspect detector documentation, diagnostic facilities and source-quality issues. Data stewards use source references to coordinate corrections with the owner of the original material, instead of silently altering its meaning.'),
    ('Institutional configuration', 'The demonstration models several mission contexts, including state policing, financial crime, cybercrime, narcotics and disaster response. These are product scenarios, not customer references. An operational installation must map its actual identities, teams, permitted purposes, sources and responsibilities to the configured system. The demonstration account picker illustrates different roles; production authentication uses the configured identity provider.')]),
article('Information integrity and control',
    'A useful analytical result must remain connected to its origin. Mosaic combines source references, explicit review and recorded handling so that an authorised reviewer can assess the material behind an action.', [
    ('Source and transformation', 'Incoming material is examined before publication. Cleaning steps, quarantined rows, duplicate handling, field mappings and proposed matches make preparation visible. Existing values, corroborating values and conflicts are distinguished in the proposed changes. At the record level, source views provide the references available for understanding a property or relationship and checking the original context.'),
    ('Human review', 'The product exposes the reasons behind proposed record matches and documented detector findings. Separate review is required by specific workflows, including intake publication and face-search confirmation. A person’s recorded decision remains distinct from the machine proposal. This distinction matters when information is incomplete, two records disagree or an apparent pattern has an ordinary explanation.'),
    ('Access and handling', 'Record access is evaluated in the context of identity, role, agency, case and purpose. Protected values can remain masked, and permitted reveal actions require a reason. A different layout, map layer or graph expansion does not create additional authority. Exports apply their release context, while retention actions distinguish eligible records from cases subject to a legal hold.'),
    ('Evidence and audit', 'Exhibit fingerprints support comparison of stored bytes with the recorded value; custody events document subsequent handling. The audit chain records activity and can be checked for integrity. Sealing a daily root and publishing an independent copy are separate steps. These mechanisms support review of handling and integrity; assessment of the original evidence still requires examination of its provenance and content.')]),
article('Deployment and integration',
    'Mosaic is an inspectable application with an explicit record model. Preparing an operational installation requires the institution’s source arrangements, identity configuration and infrastructure decisions.', [
    ('Application components', 'The interface uses React and TypeScript, with a Python FastAPI service. The application supports SQLite and PostgreSQL, and the repository is licensed under Apache 2.0. These components describe the implementation available for examination. Storage topology, capacity, backup arrangements and service operation must be selected for the intended environment.'),
    ('Identity and policy', 'Production authentication uses configured OpenID Connect sign-in; the repository includes Keycloak configuration. Policy evaluation can use Open Policy Agent or the built-in implementation. Integration requires mapping actual institutional identities, roles, agencies and case responsibilities to the application’s access model. The account-switching facility shown in the demonstration is not the production authentication process.'),
    ('Records and external services', 'Data availability depends on authorised extracts, configured connectors and source agreements. A connector does not provide automatic access to a government database. Maps use the configured basemap and geographic reference material. External map tiles and alert feeds require connectivity or an appropriate local service; offline operation depends on which resources have been supplied locally.'),
    ('Implementation considerations', 'A deployment assessment should establish the source formats, record volumes, update intervals, handling requirements, review responsibilities and retention configuration applicable to the institution. The illustrated workflows provide a basis for that assessment. The synthetic demonstration does not establish production capacity, availability commitments or performance in an operational environment.')]),
article('Document scope and reading guide',
    'This edition combines a company and product explanation with an illustrated reference to the application. Each feature chapter explains its purpose and workflow before presenting the associated screens.', [
    ('How the chapters are organised', 'Seventeen chapters follow the principal navigation areas. Each chapter introduces the input, operating process, output and relevant controls. The subsequent figure pages explain individual views, dialogs or states. The contents, PDF bookmarks and screen index provide direct navigation, allowing the document to support both a sequential product review and a focused discussion of one capability.'),
    ('How to read the figures', 'Each illustrated page contains one original screenshot. The adjacent text explains its purpose, available controls and relationship to the broader workflow. Landscape images are presented at a larger width than in the first edition; portrait report examples use a separate layout. Figures retain their source pixel dimensions in the PDF, so small interface details can be examined by zooming.'),
    ('Demonstration material', 'The screens show synthetic investigative cases and demonstration identities. Geographic reference information and public alert material can contain real place or agency names. Counts, amounts, dates and named records within the images describe the illustrated scenario. They are not claims of customer use, investigative findings, throughput, accuracy or institutional endorsement.'),
    ('Scope of the explanations', 'The text describes the available application and distinguishes operational decisions from software output. Diagnostic test-result screens and benchmark tables are excluded from the illustrations. The administration chapter explains the role of those facilities without presenting test results. Product controls support institutional procedures; the document does not represent legal advice or a certification of compliance.')], label='About this edition'),
]

def chapter(number, title, lead, blocks):
    return dict(kind='chapter', number=number, title=title, lead=lead, blocks=blocks)

CHAPTERS = [
chapter('01', 'Workspace', 'The workspace establishes the officer’s active identity, case and immediate responsibilities. It provides the common point of entry to the investigation and the decisions that require attention.', [
 ('Purpose and inputs', 'The overview combines the active case with work-queue entries relevant to the acting user. These may include leads, watched activity, saved-search results, requests, approvals and refusals. The case panel retains the investigation’s purpose, legal basis and assigned officers, so the user can verify the context before opening personal records.'),
 ('Operating workflow', 'The officer reviews the queue, filters by category or text and opens the relevant item. Global search accepts names and supported identifiers, with keyboard navigation to the selected record. Name comparison accommodates supported script and spelling variations. Opening or switching a case changes the context used by subsequent work; role-specific views expose the decisions available to an officer or supervisor.'),
 ('Outputs and controls', 'The workspace routes the user to a record, request or analytical view while retaining the active context. Restricted results and refused screens are represented explicitly. The account menu identifies the acting user and role; the demonstration picker illustrates these differences. Operational deployments use configured authentication. Work-queue times and audit times are labelled according to their respective time conventions.')]),
chapter('02', 'Case management and evidence', 'The case is the organising unit for an investigation. It connects the recorded basis for the work with the team, records, leads, exhibits, reporting and eventual outcome.', [
 ('Case development', 'The case board summarises the available network, financial activity, alerts and timeline. Evidence-based prioritisation provides reasons for examining particular people; it is scoped to the case and does not establish culpability. An officer can inspect the supporting facts, open a dossier or create a lead. The lead board records progress through investigation, approval, authorisation and closure, with supervisory control over authorisation.'),
 ('Evidence and reporting', 'Files are added as exhibits with their description, source or device details and recorded order. A SHA-256 fingerprint supports later integrity checks, while custody events document handling. The report editor provides case-diary, charge-sheet-annexure and intelligence-summary templates. Structured blocks combine records, exhibits, patterns and relationships with the officer’s narrative. Draft facts refresh from the records; an exported document is a fixed version.'),
 ('Release and lifecycle', 'Exports identify their recipient and clearance. An evidence pack can include the exhibits, custody information, draft certificate and hash manifest. The officer must review its content and release context. Closing a case records an outcome and engages its configured retention treatment. Legal holds and their removal require reasons, while eligible purge actions produce a recorded manifest. Closure, hold and deletion are separate operations.')]),
chapter('03', 'Investigative leads', 'Leads identify patterns that warrant examination. They connect a detector’s finding to the records, measurements and review steps needed to determine whether further case work is justified.', [
 ('Inputs and detection', 'The implemented detector library covers multiple mission tracks, using the record families relevant to each question. A lead can arise from financial relationships, communications, movement, assets or combinations of those sources. Detector output is constrained by the available material and its operating scope; a missing or incomplete source can change what the detector is able to identify.'),
 ('Review workflow', 'The officer filters the lead list by pattern and status, then opens a finding. The detail explains its measurements, thresholds and supporting material, together with plausible innocent explanations. The officer should inspect those records and the time interval before requesting a case or dismissing the lead. Before case approval, the workflow uses the permitted presentation of parties rather than granting unrestricted identity access.'),
 ('Outputs and decisions', 'A case request records a title, purpose and legal basis for separate supervisory consideration. The approving supervisor must satisfy the applicable agency and independence checks. Dismissal records a reason. An approved request moves the work into the authorised case context; neither the presence of a pattern nor its priority is itself a determination that an offence occurred.')]),
chapter('04', 'Financial alerts', 'Financial alerts organise typology findings for review. They bring the relevant accounts, amounts and transaction paths into view so that the officer can examine how a finding was produced.', [
 ('Purpose and inputs', 'The alert workflow applies implemented financial typologies to the available records. Different rules address different structures, including movement through accounts and relationships between entities. The result is an analytical finding with a stated rule and supporting material. Its completeness depends on the transactions and links present in the permitted case context.'),
 ('Examination process', 'The officer narrows the list by rule or severity and opens an alert. The selected finding exposes the amount, path or other measurements relevant to that typology. Links to dossiers, the money trail and the network allow the officer to inspect the parties and records from several perspectives. Dates, direction, ownership and the source of each transaction remain necessary to interpreting the pattern.'),
 ('Interpretation and follow-up', 'Severity organises attention among findings; it is not a general risk label for a person. The officer assesses the underlying material and plausible explanations before adopting a line of enquiry. An alert can inform case work or a report, but the human analysis should explain which facts support the conclusion and what additional material would be required to resolve uncertainty.')]),
chapter('05', 'Watches and saved searches', 'Watches retain an investigative question across changes in the available data. They return relevant activity to the work queue so that the officer can review new material in its case context.', [
 ('Watch scope', 'An entity watch follows activity associated with a selected record. A place watch describes a geographic area and the relevant context for monitoring it. The officer records a reason and chooses the supported history interval. Activity is then presented with its kind and importance, allowing the user to examine the underlying event rather than relying on an unexplained notification.'),
 ('Review workflow', 'The watchlist provides access to current watches and their resulting activity. Filters narrow the items requiring attention, and reviewed status distinguishes material already considered. Stopping a watch is an explicit action with a reason. The work queue brings relevant updates back into the officer’s daily workflow without requiring the original analytical view to remain open.'),
 ('Saved queries and limits', 'Explorer searches can also be saved with a reason and subsequently re-evaluated as the work queue loads. This retains a repeatable query as well as a selected object or area. The capability operates on records available to Mosaic; it does not imply continuous access to external feeds. An update indicates relevant available material, which the officer must still examine for significance and reliability.')]),
chapter('06', 'Network analysis', 'The network presents records and relationships as an interactive graph. It supports structural examination while preserving the distinction between recorded links, analytical measures and investigative conclusions.', [
 ('Exploration', 'The officer selects records, expands their neighbours and switches between layouts suited to structure, money flow, record types or communities. Filters narrow relationship classes and object families. Labels, spacing, zoom, search and the record list make a complex view more manageable. Hiding an item changes the working view; it does not delete the underlying record.'),
 ('Analytical methods', 'Analysis of the loaded graph includes communities, betweenness, articulation points, k-cores, connected components, directed cycles and bridges. Path tools can examine undirected, directed and money-only connections, including alternative paths. These methods answer structural questions about the material currently loaded. For example, a bridge can identify a connection whose removal separates parts of that graph.'),
 ('Interpretation and scope', 'A network measure describes the graph, not a person’s intent. A cycle containing different relationship types is not necessarily a circular movement of money, and maximum flow is not a statement of actual transferred value. The officer should inspect edge types and supporting records before drawing conclusions. Expansion and layout changes remain subject to the active permissions and cannot widen the user’s authority.')]),
chapter('07', 'Entity dossier', 'The dossier consolidates the authorised information associated with a selected entity. It provides a structured reading of identity, activity, relationships and provenance without requiring the officer to rebuild that context in each tool.', [
 ('Record overview', 'The overview presents the available identifying details, original-script names, family information, addresses and source references. Case-specific priority and its reasons can direct attention to relevant facts. Actions connect the record to leads, watches, the network and financial tracing. Masked fields remain visibly distinct from values available to the acting officer.'),
 ('Activity and relationships', 'Dedicated views organise financial activity, communication records, travel, assets, relationships and chronological events. Financial filters can narrow accounts and amounts; transaction rows retain direction, channel, narration and counterparties. Communications summaries provide contacts and timing. The network and timeline place the record’s activity in relation to other objects and events, with paging where the record set requires it.'),
 ('Provenance and controls', 'The sources view identifies available property origins, ingestion context and original rows. The officer should use these references to assess conflicting or incomplete information. A permitted unmask action requires a reason and cannot reveal a complete value that was never stored. The dossier assembles the records the case may read; it is neither a complete biography nor evidence that all relevant sources have been obtained.')]),
chapter('08', 'Financial transaction tracing', 'Money trail follows directed financial relationships from a selected person, company or account. It helps the officer examine origin, onward movement and endpoints within the available transaction records.', [
 ('Starting conditions', 'The officer selects a starting entity and chooses the direction of examination. An outward trace follows funds sent onwards; an inward trace examines the available origins. Hop limits and minimum amounts constrain the displayed result. The choice of party-level or account-level presentation changes the analytical granularity without changing the underlying evidence.'),
 ('Reading the trace', 'Directed links represent the financial path exposed by the query. Selecting or hovering over a component reveals the associated context, while endpoint actions connect to dossiers and further analysis. The officer can examine foreign endpoints, cash withdrawals and retail originators or recipients where those records are present. Direction and level of aggregation must be read together to interpret the displayed movement correctly.'),
 ('Outputs and limitations', 'The result provides a path for examination and a route to the underlying records. A trace is bounded by the selected limits and the data available to the case. The end of a displayed path may reflect missing records or a query boundary, rather than the final destination of funds. The officer must substantiate any narrative about source, control or purpose through the relevant transaction and ownership evidence.')]),
chapter('09', 'Relationship examination', 'Connections supports a focused enquiry about one record’s associates or the paths between two records. It complements the broader network with a narrower question and a readable chain of relationships.', [
 ('Inputs', 'The enquiry begins with a selected record. The officer can inspect available associates or specify a second endpoint to examine a relationship between the two. Candidate endpoints can be selected through the search and associated-record controls. The active case and permissions continue to determine which records and relationships are available.'),
 ('Workflow', 'The view presents the relationship structure and its supporting context. The officer examines the sequence of records, opens a dossier when more detail is needed and checks the evidence for each step. Inferred associations require particular attention to their stated basis. Clearing the second endpoint returns the view to exploration from the original record.'),
 ('Use of the result', 'A connection identifies a route through the available record model. It does not by itself establish collaboration, knowledge or intent between the endpoints. The officer should distinguish direct recorded interactions from longer chains or inferred associations, and describe those differences when using the result in a case narrative or deciding what additional evidence to seek.')]),
chapter('10', 'Geographic investigation', 'Map combines records and relationships with geographic context. Area search examines observations within a defined place and period, supporting a more precise question than proximity on a general map.', [
 ('Geographic overview', 'The map can display the active case or other permitted flagged material. Layers represent people, companies, cases, financial movement, relationships and travel. Clusters and zoom provide a route from a regional overview to a local view. Selecting an item exposes its context and provides links to the dossier, trace or network where available.'),
 ('Area enquiry', 'The officer chooses a place, camera, tower or map point, then defines a radius, time interval and reason. Results distinguish the available vehicle, phone and travel observations and show their recorded times and locations. Owner records, dossiers and networks support follow-up. The area can also inform a watch, saved search or report where the workflow provides those actions.'),
 ('Interpretation', 'A registered address does not establish physical presence at a particular time. An observed device or vehicle must be interpreted according to the source and its connection to a person. Inferred relationships are distinct from recorded movement. Areas without an applicable source are identified so that an absence of observations is not mistaken for evidence that no activity occurred.')]),
chapter('11', 'Record exploration', 'Explorer provides direct access to the permitted record model. It supports both finding a particular object and narrowing a larger collection through structured filters and summaries.', [
 ('Record inspection', 'Search and type filters narrow the available objects. Selecting a result opens its properties, relationships, handling context and provenance. The officer can use a known identifier or inspect the information associated with an object before moving to a dossier or another analytical view. The result remains subject to the same access restrictions as the rest of the workspace.'),
 ('Aggregate exploration', 'The police overview summarises incidents by dimensions such as month, district, section and kind. Selecting a summary narrows the underlying result set, allowing the officer to move from a broad distribution to the relevant records. These summaries describe the loaded material and the applied filters; they should be interpreted with regard to source coverage and update timing.'),
 ('Continuation of work', 'Where permitted, an officer can add a record to the active case or request a protected value with a reason. A search can be saved so the same question can be revisited through the work queue. Explorer therefore acts as both a direct record reference and an entry point into case work, while preserving the query and handling context behind the selection.')]),
chapter('12', 'Data preparation and publication', 'Data studio makes the transition from incoming extract to shared case records explicit. Preparation, proposed changes and approval are separate stages, allowing a reviewer to examine the effect before publication.', [
 ('Prepare and map', 'The officer chooses the permitted source and case context. Profiling examines values, and an ordered cleaning recipe records transformations, quarantined rows and duplicate treatment. Field mapping assigns rows and columns to record types, properties and relationships. Policy-blocked classes and unmapped fields remain visible. The source material remains the reference for assessing what has changed.'),
 ('Match and inspect', 'Rows are classified as existing, probable or new, with the basis for the match. Checks expose patterns and conflicts with possible ordinary explanations. Proposed operations distinguish new records, filled gaps, corroboration, conflicts and learned spellings. Narrative material can be tagged for identifiers, dates, amounts and places; a name-to-person association requires supporting identifiers on the same page.'),
 ('Submit and review', 'Submission freezes the proposal with its order and explanatory note. A different eligible supervisor in the case reviews the operations and records an approval or rejection reason. Approval publishes the proposed changes. A later revert follows the recorded publication workflow, and pattern analysis can be rerun over the resulting records. The separation of preparation from approval makes the decision and its scope inspectable.')]),
chapter('13', 'Identity-match review', 'Review matches addresses uncertain record identity. It gives a person the evidence needed to decide whether two source records refer to the same individual, instead of treating name similarity as sufficient.', [
 ('Comparison inputs', 'A waiting pair presents the original names and scripts, family information, birth details, district and available identifiers. The explanation identifies why the records were paired and which fields agree or differ. The weight of a common name differs from that of a more distinctive one, helping the reviewer interpret similarity in context.'),
 ('Review process', 'The reviewer selects a pair, compares the source details and examines the explanation field by field. Conflicting information should be considered alongside agreement, including whether the sources have different dates or levels of completeness. The interface provides an explicit same-person or different-people decision with a recorded reason; simply opening the pair does not accept the proposal.'),
 ('Decision record', 'The outcome records the reviewer’s interpretation of the available evidence and enables the corresponding record treatment. It should be specific enough to explain why the evidence supports or rejects the association. The implementation includes reversal support for match decisions; this document does not imply an undo control on a screen where none is shown. Subsequent source corrections may require the decision to be reconsidered.')]),
chapter('14', 'Face-search review', 'Face search is a case-bound comparison workflow. It links the probe image to evidence handling, presents candidate photographs with their sources and separates the initial search from the confirmation decision.', [
 ('Evidence and selection', 'The officer selects a still and records the order or seizure context under which it was obtained. The workflow adds the image as case evidence before detecting faces. If several faces are present, the officer selects the intended subject and records a reason for the search. An image with no detected face requires a more suitable probe rather than an unsupported comparison.'),
 ('Candidate examination', 'The selected face is compared with photographs available to the active case. Results display candidate similarity and the source of the photograph, allowing the officer to examine the context behind the resemblance. A candidate is a suggested comparison, not an established identity. Image quality, source context and other available evidence remain relevant to the assessment.'),
 ('Independent decision', 'Another officer of the same case reviews the proposed match and records confirmation or rejection with a reason. The searching officer cannot confirm their own search. The resulting record preserves the human decision separately from the comparison output. Confirmation represents a recorded review action and should be supported by the evidence considered, rather than inferred solely from a similarity value.')]),
chapter('15', 'Disaster-response operations', 'The disaster map organises ingested public alerts with geographic reference data and recorded resource allocations. It provides a control-room view of the alert, its affected area and the response entered in Mosaic.', [
 ('Alerts and geography', 'The view combines ingested SACHET alert material with district boundaries, hospitals and relevant reference data. The alert’s sender, severity, effective time and expiry remain part of the displayed context. Mapping can use an official district code, a polygon or circle, a place name or river-gauge information. Alerts that cannot be mapped remain available in the list.'),
 ('Operational examination', 'The operator selects an alert, checks its timing and mapping basis, and examines the affected districts. Available river levels and warning or danger marks provide additional context for flood material. Nearby hospitals support resource planning. The map and list should be read together, particularly when geography is derived from names rather than an explicit alert boundary.'),
 ('Dispatch record', 'A dispatch entry identifies the resource, quantity and destination district. The log makes recorded allocations visible against the disaster context. This is a record of the action entered in the application, not confirmation of physical delivery. Alert freshness depends on ingestion; the demonstration does not establish a continuously live operational feed or an end-to-end emergency response service.')]),
chapter('16', 'Oversight and retention', 'Oversight provides a separate view of system activity. It enables review of who performed an action, in which case and for what purpose, while keeping the investigative workspace outside the auditor’s role.', [
 ('Audit examination', 'Each event records the actor, time, action, case, purpose, record count and outcome. User and record filters support a focused question about access. Refused requests remain part of the trail. Insider-threat alerts identify activity requiring examination, such as unusual bulk access, but require contextual review before an organisational conclusion is reached.'),
 ('Integrity and transparency', 'The audit chain can be verified against its recorded state. A daily root can be sealed; publishing an independent copy outside the application is a separate operational action that provides a reference for later comparison. Public transparency information summarises activity by dimensions such as action, outcome, purpose and source, without exposing the detailed investigative record workspace.'),
 ('Retention administration', 'The retention panel distinguishes due, scheduled and held cases. Legal holds preserve their recorded reason. Eligible administrators and supervisors can run the applicable purge action, which records completed work and manifest references. Retention should therefore be read as a sequence of configured eligibility, explicit action and recorded outcome, with held cases treated separately.')]),
chapter('17', 'Product administration', 'Administration makes detector behaviour and source-quality issues available for inspection. It supports maintainers and data stewards in understanding how the analytical functions operate and where input material requires attention.', [
 ('Detector documentation', 'The library describes the question addressed by each detector, its method, supported record types, mission tracks and scope. Expanded entries explain tactics, possible evasion, ordinary look-alikes, limitations and references. This context helps a reviewer understand why a finding may appear and which conditions limit its interpretation. Version information identifies the documented implementation.'),
 ('Diagnostic facilities', 'Pattern tests and record-matching diagnostics are maintainer tools for examining implementation behaviour. They serve a different purpose from an investigator’s case findings. This publication describes their place in the product but excludes their screenshots, benchmark tables and test-result values. No demonstrated test output is presented as a promise of operational accuracy or performance.'),
 ('Source-quality management', 'The source-quality register associates an issue with an affected record and its originating source. A data steward can use those references to inspect the extract and coordinate a correction with its owner. The register is a reporting and investigation aid, not a bulk editor that silently changes source records. Correcting an input and assessing its downstream effects remain explicit operational responsibilities.')]),
]
